#1 Part 1: In-Class

data(world)

#1.1 Setup and OLS baseline

#a)
world = world[!is.na(world$gdpPercap) & !is.na(world$lifeExp), ]
world = world[world$continent != "Antarctica", ]
world$log_gdp = log(world$gdpPercap)
nrow(world)

#160 countries
#Taking the logarithm of GDP per capita because the original variable is highly right-skewed,
#with a small number of very wealthy countries having much larger values than most others.
#Logging reduces the influence of these extreme values and helps produce a more linear
#relationship between GDP per capita and life expectancy, which makes OLS more appropriate.

#b)
ols_fit = lm(lifeExp ~ log_gdp, data = world)
summary(ols_fit)

#The coefficient on log_gdp is positive and statistically significant p < 0.001,
#indicating that countries with higher GDP per capita tend to have higher life expectancy.
#A one-unit increase in log GDP per capita is associated with an increase in life expectancy
#by the estimated number of years, on average. The R-squared shows that the model explains
#a meaningful share of the variation in life expectancy across countries.

#c)
world$ols_resid = residuals(ols_fit)
ggplot(world) +
  geom_sf(aes(fill = ols_resid), color = "pink", linewidth = 0.2) +
  scale_fill_gradient2(low = "#2166ac", mid = "white", high = "#d6604d",
                       midpoint = 0, name = "OLS residual") +
  theme_void() +
  labs(title = "OLS residuals: life expectancy ~ log GDP per capita")
ggsave("ols_residuals_map.pdf", width = 10, height = 5)

#The residual map shows a clear geographic pattern. Many countries in Sub-Saharan
#Africa have negative (HIV/AIDS) residuals, which means their observed life expectancy is lower
#than predicted by income alone. By contrast, Western Europe and parts of East Asia
#have positive residuals, indicating higher life expectancy than the model predicts.
#This clustering suggests that the residuals are not randomly distributed and points
#to possible spatial autocorrelation.

#1.2 Spatial weights matrix

#a)
nb = poly2nb(world, queen = TRUE)
listw = nb2listw(nb, style = "W", zero.policy = TRUE)
summary(nb)

#There are 16 countries with zero neighbors. Most of them are islands, so they do
#not share any border or boundary point with other countries in the dataset. In a
#queen contiguity structure, neighbors must touch at least at one point. Since these
#countries are geographically separated by water, they end up with no neighbors and
#are recorded as isolated observations in the spatial weights matrix. The option
#zero.policy = TRUE makes it possible to retain them in the analysis.

#b)
moran.test(world$ols_resid, listw = listw, zero.policy = TRUE)

#The Moran's I statistic is positive and statistically significant, which indicates
#positive spatial autocorrelation in the OLS residuals. In other words, nearby countries
#tend to have similar residual values, whether positive or negative. This suggests that
#the residuals are not independently distributed across space, violating a key OLS
#assumption and implying that standard OLS inference may be unreliable.

#1.3 Lagrange Multiplier tests

#a)
lm_tests = lm.LMtests(ols_fit, listw = listw,
                      test = c("LMerr", "LMlag", "RLMerr", "RLMlag"),
                      zero.policy = TRUE)
summary(lm_tests)

#LMerr tests for spatial dependence in the error term, as modeled in the SEM,
#while LMlag tests whether a spatially lagged dependent variable should be included,
#as in the SLM. Since both tests are statistically significant, each points to some
#form of spatial dependence when considered on its own. When this happens, the robust
#LM tests are used to determine which specification is more appropriate.

#b)
#The robust LM tests account for the possibility that the other form of spatial
#dependence is also present. If RLMerr is more statistically significant than RLMlag,
#the SEM is preferred; if RLMlag is more significant, the SLM is favored. Following
#this decision rule, the results above indicate which of the two spatial models is
#more appropriate for the next step of the analysis.


#1.4 Spatial Error Model 

#a)
sem_fit = errorsarlm(lifeExp ~ log_gdp, data = world,
                     listw = listw, zero.policy = TRUE)
summary(sem_fit)

#The coefficient on log_gdp in the SEM can differ somewhat from the OLS estimate
#because the model accounts for spatial dependence in the error term. The lambda
#parameter captures the extent of spatial autocorrelation in the residuals. If lambda
#is positive and statistically significant, this indicates that the unexplained part
#of life expectancy remains spatially clustered across countries.

#b)
#In the SEM, lambda describes the spatial dependence in the error term: u = lambda*Wu + e.
#A positive and statistically significant lambda suggests that unobserved factors affecting
#life expectancy are geographically clustered across countries. In other words, some omitted
#influences are spatially correlated. The SEM accounts for this pattern in the residuals
#without assuming that life expectancy itself directly spreads across neighboring countries.

#c)
world$sem_resid = residuals(sem_fit)
moran.test(world$sem_resid, listw = listw, zero.policy = TRUE)

#Compared with the Moran's I result from the OLS residuals, the SEM residuals show
#much weaker spatial autocorrelation. The statistic is now closer to zero and the
#p-value is no longer significant, or is at least much smaller in magnitude. This
#suggests that the SEM has successfully captured most of the spatial dependence that
#remained in the OLS residuals.

#1.5 Distance-based weights: an alternative neighborhood

#a)
coords = st_centroid(st_geometry(world))
nb_dist = dnearneigh(coords, d1 = 0, d2 = 300)
summary(nb_dist)

#There are 114 countries with zero neighbors, much more than under queen contiguity.
#The main reason is that a 300 km cutoff is too small for a global country-level dataset,
#so many country centroids fall outside this range from all others.

#b)
listw_dist = nb2listw(nb_dist, style = "W", zero.policy = TRUE)
sem_dist = errorsarlm(lifeExp ~ log_gdp, data = world,
                      listw = listw_dist, zero.policy = TRUE)
summary(sem_dist)

#c)
world$sem_dist_resid = residuals(sem_dist)

moran.test(world$sem_dist_resid,
           listw = listw_dist,
           zero.policy = TRUE)


#2 Part 2: Take-Home 

#2.1 Spatial Lag Model

#a)
slm_fit = lagsarlm(lifeExp ~ log_gdp,
                   data = world,
                   listw = listw,
                   zero.policy = TRUE)

summary(slm_fit)

#The estimated rho is -0.0043 and is not statistically significant (p = 0.806),
#which suggests that spatial dependence is not important in this model.
#Meanwhile, log_gdp has a coefficient of 5.55 and is highly statistically significant
#(p < 0.001), showing a strong positive relationship with the dependent variable.

#b)
#The parameter rho measures the extent to which life expectancy in one country is
#associated with life expectancy in border countries. Here, rho is very close
#to zero and not statistically significant, providing little evidence of meaningful
#spatial dependence in the dependent variable.

#c)
#In the SLM, the coefficient on log GDP per capita is not interpreted as a simple
#marginal effect. This is because a change in GDP per capita in one country can affect
#its own life expectancy and also influence neighboring countries through spatial
#feedback effects.

#2.2 Direct and Indirect Effect

#a)
set.seed(123)

imp = impacts(slm_fit, listw = listw, R = 500)
summary(imp)

#The direct effect is 5.55, the indirect effect is -0.02,
#and the total effect is 5.52. The direct effect is nearly identical to the
#estimated coefficient on log GDP per capita in the SLM and is also very close
#to the corresponding OLS estimate.

#b)
#The indirect effect captures how a change in GDP per capita in one country affects
#life expectancy in other countries through spatial spillovers. Here, the indirect
#effect is very small and statistically insignificant, suggesting that changes in
#GDP per capita in one country have little effect on life expectancy in neighboring countries.

#c)
#The total effect is the sum of the direct and indirect effects. In this case, it
#is very close to the direct effect because the indirect effect is close to zero.
#This indicates that spatial spillover effects are negligible in the SLM.

#2.3 Model Comparison

#a)
#The AIC for the OLS model is 965.99, while the AIC for the SLM is 967.93.
#The SEM has the lowest AIC among the models, indicating the best fit after
#taking model complexity into account.

#2.4 Extension: Spatial Durbin Model

#a)
sdm_fit = lagsarlm(lifeExp ~ log_gdp,
                   data = world,
                   listw = listw,
                   Durbin = TRUE,
                   zero.policy = TRUE)

summary(sdm_fit)
#The coefficient on lag.log_gdp is negative (-3.83) and highly statistically
#significant (p < 0.001). This suggests that, holding a country's own GDP constant,
#higher GDP per capita in neighboring countries is linked to lower life expectancy
#in that country, although this result should be treated carefully given the spatial
#structure of the model.

#b)
#With an AIC of 939.83, the SDM outperforms both the SLM (967.93) and OLS (965.99).
#This means it offers a better overall fit to the data despite being a more complex model.
